WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.625
Welcome. This class treats AI-assisted
writing as a controlled transformation,

2
00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:11.250
not permission to invent. You will define
what may change,

3
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.875
what must remain exact, and who accepts
the result. You

4
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:22.500
will separate source extraction from
rewriting, compare the output with

5
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.125
the original, correct drift without
destabilizing the whole document, and

6
00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:33.750
protect authors, subjects, and readers.
The test of success is

7
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:39.375
not whether the new text sounds polished.
It is whether

8
00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:45.000
the intended meaning survives and the
result can be reviewed.

9
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.923
Begin with a transformation brief. Name
the audience, purpose, channel,

10
00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:58.846
length, reading level, and review owner.
Identify the transformation:

11
00:00:58.846 --> 00:01:05.769
summarization, restructuring, translation,
editing, or new drafting. Point to the

12
00:01:05.769 --> 00:01:12.692
source of truth. List names, dates,
numbers, quotations, obligations,

13
00:01:12.692 --> 00:01:19.615
technical terms, conclusions, and voice
elements that must remain exact.

14
00:01:19.615 --> 00:01:26.538
List structure, length, tone, or
vocabulary that should change. State

15
00:01:26.538 --> 00:01:33.462
what the output must not add. Define
acceptance checks for

16
00:01:33.462 --> 00:01:40.385
meaning, evidence, accessibility,
permission, and approval. Remove personal,

17
00:01:40.385 --> 00:01:47.308
confidential, licensed, or restricted
material that the task does not

18
00:01:47.308 --> 00:01:54.231
need. Confirm that the selected service
and reviewers are authorized

19
00:01:54.231 --> 00:02:01.154
to handle what remains. A useful brief
creates two visible

20
00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:08.077
boundaries: freedom to improve
presentation inside the brief, and no

21
00:02:08.077 --> 00:02:15.000
authority to change protected meaning or
manufacture facts.

22
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.417
Suppose I need to turn a technical
incident summary into

23
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:25.833
a short customer update. The source says
service was unavailable

24
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:31.250
for twenty-three minutes, recovery
completed at fourteen thirty UTC, and

25
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:36.667
the cause remains under investigation. I
preserve the duration, timestamp,

26
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:42.083
status, and uncertainty exactly. I may
replace internal headings, shorten

27
00:02:42.083 --> 00:02:47.500
implementation detail, and explain
unfamiliar terms. I prohibit a root-cause

28
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.917
claim, apology on behalf of a named
executive, or promise

29
00:02:52.917 --> 00:02:58.333
about future reliability unless an
authorized source supplies it. The

30
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:03.750
owner of customer communications reviews
the result. This brief allows

31
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:09.167
clearer language while preventing a
plausible but unsupported sentence such

32
00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:14.583
as, the issue was caused by a network
failure and

33
00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:20.000
cannot happen again.

34
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.429
Execute in visible passes. In pass one,
extract source facts,

35
00:03:26.429 --> 00:03:32.857
required terms, quoted language,
structure, and explicit unknowns. Do not

36
00:03:32.857 --> 00:03:39.286
improve the prose yet. In pass two,
transform only within

37
00:03:39.286 --> 00:03:45.714
the brief. Keep source-derived statements
separate from proposed additions. If

38
00:03:45.714 --> 00:03:52.143
an addition could help, label it Proposed
and require evidence

39
00:03:52.143 --> 00:03:58.571
or owner approval before it enters the
draft. For long

40
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:05.000
work, process bounded sections and keep a
change log that

41
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.429
records what moved, shortened, clarified,
or remained exact. Provide enough

42
00:04:11.429 --> 00:04:17.857
neighboring context to preserve meaning,
but not unrelated sensitive material.

43
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:24.286
Do not ask a model to imitate a living

44
00:04:24.286 --> 00:04:30.714
person's distinctive voice. Describe
approved voice characteristics such as

45
00:04:30.714 --> 00:04:37.143
direct, calm, concise, or instructional.
Visible passes make it easier

46
00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:43.571
to locate where an unsupported detail
entered and to compare

47
00:04:43.571 --> 00:04:50.000
the output without relying on memory.

48
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.250
Choose a permitted source passage. Mark
one sentence, value, or

49
00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:02.500
obligation that must remain exact. Mark
one structural or stylistic

50
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:08.750
element that may change. Write one detail
the model must

51
00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:15.000
not add.

52
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.786
Verify the transformation against both
source and brief. Compare every

53
00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:58.571
name, date, number, quotation, obligation,
condition, exception, and conclusion.

54
00:06:58.571 --> 00:07:05.357
Check whether simplification changed who
must act, what is allowed,

55
00:07:05.357 --> 00:07:12.143
when a rule applies, or how certain the
source was.

56
00:07:12.143 --> 00:07:18.929
Map any new factual statement to evidence.
Remove citation-shaped text

57
00:07:18.929 --> 00:07:25.714
until the original has been opened and
checked. Read as

58
00:07:25.714 --> 00:07:32.500
the intended audience. Confirm logical
heading order, meaningful links, defined

59
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:39.286
abbreviations, plain language, inclusive
terminology, keyboard and screen-reader

60
00:07:39.286 --> 00:07:46.071
clarity, and any required disclosure of AI
assistance. Read difficult

61
00:07:46.071 --> 00:07:52.857
passages aloud or use text-to-speech to
catch missing words and

62
00:07:52.857 --> 00:07:59.643
overloaded sentences. For consequential
legal, policy, safety, financial, or

63
00:07:59.643 --> 00:08:06.429
reputational language, route the exact
source and transformed passage to

64
00:08:06.429 --> 00:08:13.214
the accountable reviewer. Readability is
valuable, but it cannot override

65
00:08:13.214 --> 00:08:20.000
meaning.

66
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:27.000
Checkpoint. The source says an
administrator must approve access before

67
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:34.000
use. The plain-language draft says an
administrator can review access

68
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:41.000
after use. The second sentence is shorter.
Is it acceptable,

69
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:48.000
and what should happen next?

70
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.444
Reject the draft. Must approve before use
is an obligation

71
00:09:52.444 --> 00:09:56.889
and timing condition. Can review after use
changes both. Return

72
00:09:56.889 --> 00:10:01.333
to the exact source passage, restate the
preservation rule, correct

73
00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:05.778
only the affected section, and obtain the
responsible review. If

74
00:10:05.778 --> 00:10:10.222
you accepted the rewrite because it was
easier to read,

75
00:10:10.222 --> 00:10:14.667
revise your criterion: plain language must
preserve who acts, what

76
00:10:14.667 --> 00:10:19.111
is required, and when. If you regenerated
the entire document,

77
00:10:19.111 --> 00:10:23.556
consider the smaller repair. A bounded
correction reduces the chance

78
00:10:23.556 --> 00:10:28.000
of introducing new drift elsewhere.

79
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:34.154
Recover from drift without hiding it.
Place the accepted source

80
00:10:34.154 --> 00:10:40.308
and current output side by side. Identify
the smallest affected

81
00:10:40.308 --> 00:10:46.462
section and classify the failure: changed
meaning, invented detail, omitted

82
00:10:46.462 --> 00:10:52.615
limitation, altered voice, unsupported
quotation, or sensitive-data exposure.

83
00:10:52.615 --> 00:10:58.769
Return to the exact source passage and
brief rule. Correct

84
00:10:58.769 --> 00:11:04.923
only the affected section, then rerun the
local audit and

85
00:11:04.923 --> 00:11:11.077
any dependent cross-document checks.
Record the detected drift, likely cause,

86
00:11:11.077 --> 00:11:17.231
correction, and reviewer. If an invented
detail entered because the

87
00:11:17.231 --> 00:11:23.385
brief was silent, add an explicit
prohibited-addition rule. If source

88
00:11:23.385 --> 00:11:29.538
material was inappropriate to process,
stop, remove it from the

89
00:11:29.538 --> 00:11:35.692
workflow according to policy, and involve
the data owner. Escalate

90
00:11:35.692 --> 00:11:41.846
claims whose legal, safety, policy, or
reputational meaning cannot be

91
00:11:41.846 --> 00:11:48.000
resolved by the writing team.

92
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.071
Protect authors, subjects, and readers.
Minimize sensitive material before

93
00:11:54.071 --> 00:12:00.143
generation and confirm permission,
confidentiality, copyright, license,

94
00:12:00.143 --> 00:12:06.214
translation, and attribution requirements.
Do not treat public availability as

95
00:12:06.214 --> 00:12:12.286
permission for every reuse. Avoid
deceptive impersonation and undisclosed

96
00:12:12.286 --> 00:12:18.357
synthetic quotations. Never publish a
generated quotation or citation without

97
00:12:18.357 --> 00:12:24.429
checking the original. Preserve
attribution when the transformation

98
00:12:24.429 --> 00:12:30.500
depends on another person's work. Record
the source version, brief

99
00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:36.571
version, generated draft, accepted
changes, limitations, reviewer, and final

100
00:12:36.571 --> 00:12:42.643
disposition. Delete or retain intermediate
material according to policy rather

101
00:12:42.643 --> 00:12:48.714
than convenience. Disclose AI assistance
when policy, context, or reader

102
00:12:48.714 --> 00:12:54.786
expectations require it. A reproducible
correction record should show what

103
00:12:54.786 --> 00:13:00.857
drifted, why it mattered, how it was
fixed, and who

104
00:13:00.857 --> 00:13:06.929
accepted the result. Transformation is
successful only when meaning, permission,

105
00:13:06.929 --> 00:13:13.000
and accountability survive.

106
00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:18.625
Open the reviewed transformation activity.
Choose a permitted

107
00:13:18.625 --> 00:13:24.250
three-hundred-to-five-hundred-word source.
Define audience, purpose, transformation

108
00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:29.875
type, exact preservation rules, permitted
changes, and prohibited additions. Build

109
00:13:29.875 --> 00:13:35.500
pass one as extracted facts and structure,
then pass two

110
00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:41.125
as audience-ready prose. Audit meaning,
evidence, accessibility, attribution, and

111
00:13:41.125 --> 00:13:46.750
sensitive data. Introduce one deliberate
drift error, detect it, correct

112
00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:52.375
only the affected section, and save the
cause, fix, and

113
00:13:52.375 --> 00:13:58.000
reviewer record.

114
00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.167
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

115
00:17:02.167 --> 00:17:06.333
identify a complete transformation brief,
explain visible writing passes, respond

116
00:17:06.333 --> 00:17:10.500
to a changed obligation, protect sensitive
source material, and recognize

117
00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:14.667
a reproducible correction record. Review
the class or return to

118
00:17:14.667 --> 00:17:18.833
the activity before submitting. The
assessment begins only when you

119
00:17:18.833 --> 00:17:23.000
choose Begin knowledge check.

120
00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:28.000
Keep the boundary visible: transform
presentation, not evidence or obligation.

121
00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:33.000
Preserve meaning, verify every
consequential change, and leave a

122
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:38.000
